WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.137 --> 00:00:03.060
- A hole in a plane,
government investigations,

00:00:03.060 --> 00:00:04.297
aircraft being grounded,

00:00:04.297 --> 00:00:06.690
$12 billion wiped from the stock price

00:00:06.690 --> 00:00:08.670
and airlines reexamining their aircraft

00:00:08.670 --> 00:00:11.610
to find yet more manufacturing faults.

00:00:11.610 --> 00:00:14.670
This wasn't exactly the start
to 2024 that Boeing wanted.

00:00:14.670 --> 00:00:16.620
See, after spending years
enduring the fallout

00:00:16.620 --> 00:00:19.170
from the MAX crisis where
two Boeing planes crashed

00:00:19.170 --> 00:00:21.090
due to a flight control design flaw

00:00:21.090 --> 00:00:22.560
killing hundreds of people,

00:00:22.560 --> 00:00:25.830
the company was finally showing
signs of a full recovery.

00:00:25.830 --> 00:00:28.680
- They had normalized production levels,

00:00:28.680 --> 00:00:31.140
they were getting some more
respect on Wall Street.

00:00:31.140 --> 00:00:32.280
- But then a door plug

00:00:32.280 --> 00:00:34.290
from an Alaska Airlines Boeing aircraft,

00:00:34.290 --> 00:00:36.570
which had only been
delivered months before,

00:00:36.570 --> 00:00:38.070
fell off in midair,

00:00:38.070 --> 00:00:40.500
triggering yet another
crisis for the company.

00:00:40.500 --> 00:00:43.080
- What does Boeing look
like in the next few years

00:00:43.080 --> 00:00:45.120
if it has to deal with another crisis?

00:00:45.120 --> 00:00:47.643
- [George] So can Boeing
afford another setback?

00:00:50.550 --> 00:00:52.950
While nobody was killed
or seriously injured

00:00:52.950 --> 00:00:54.600
in the Alaska Airlines accident-

00:00:54.600 --> 00:00:57.594
- We coulda ended up with
something so much more tragic

00:00:57.594 --> 00:01:01.920
and we're really fortunate
that that did not occur here.

00:01:01.920 --> 00:01:02.753
- [George] The door plug,

00:01:02.753 --> 00:01:04.590
which is a panel used to plug up the space

00:01:04.590 --> 00:01:06.420
where an emergency exit would be,

00:01:06.420 --> 00:01:08.340
fell off early in the flight.

00:01:08.340 --> 00:01:10.170
- It was shortly after takeoff,

00:01:10.170 --> 00:01:12.570
passengers were still in their seatbelt,

00:01:12.570 --> 00:01:13.830
the plane was ascending

00:01:13.830 --> 00:01:15.480
and there was no one seated

00:01:15.480 --> 00:01:18.240
right next to the door plug that blew off.

00:01:18.240 --> 00:01:20.010
- If somebody had been in that seat,

00:01:20.010 --> 00:01:22.290
it's likely they probably would've died.

00:01:22.290 --> 00:01:25.110
That's at least what
we're told by the NTSB.

00:01:25.110 --> 00:01:28.290
And that's really what's
triggered this massive panic

00:01:28.290 --> 00:01:30.930
and investigation into
what actually happened.

00:01:30.930 --> 00:01:31.890
- [George] One major question

00:01:31.890 --> 00:01:33.630
in the accident investigator's probe

00:01:33.630 --> 00:01:35.400
is whether this was a one-off accident

00:01:35.400 --> 00:01:36.900
for the singular door plug,

00:01:36.900 --> 00:01:37.964
or a manufacturing issue

00:01:37.964 --> 00:01:40.680
that could affect many
of Boeing's aircraft.

00:01:40.680 --> 00:01:42.120
The door plugs are manufactured

00:01:42.120 --> 00:01:44.400
by a company called Spirit Aerosystems,

00:01:44.400 --> 00:01:46.080
which actually used to be part of Boeing

00:01:46.080 --> 00:01:48.930
until the division was spun off in 2005.

00:01:48.930 --> 00:01:50.550
Spirit has said it's working with Boeing

00:01:50.550 --> 00:01:51.960
to determine what went wrong,

00:01:51.960 --> 00:01:53.120
but a crucial piece of evidence,

00:01:53.120 --> 00:01:56.160
the bolts that are supposed to
hold the door plug in place,

00:01:56.160 --> 00:01:57.270
remain missing,

00:01:57.270 --> 00:02:00.690
- And we have not yet determined
if they existed there.

00:02:00.690 --> 00:02:02.340
- Figuring out what
happened with the bolts

00:02:02.340 --> 00:02:04.050
is a key piece of this.

00:02:04.050 --> 00:02:05.940
- The accident led to the FAA grounding

00:02:05.940 --> 00:02:08.490
around 170 MAX 9 aircraft

00:02:08.490 --> 00:02:10.500
and ordering the inspection of the planes.

00:02:10.500 --> 00:02:12.660
But it wasn't long before
the reputational damage

00:02:12.660 --> 00:02:16.020
to Boeing and the FAA
began to spread further

00:02:16.020 --> 00:02:17.640
than the MAX 9's door plugs.

00:02:17.640 --> 00:02:20.250
See, when it comes to
recommendations, inspections,

00:02:20.250 --> 00:02:21.660
and safety checks for Boeing

00:02:21.660 --> 00:02:23.370
and other American made aircraft,

00:02:23.370 --> 00:02:25.440
airlines and regulators across the globe

00:02:25.440 --> 00:02:27.660
typically follow the lead of the FAA.

00:02:27.660 --> 00:02:29.280
However, around the same time

00:02:29.280 --> 00:02:31.770
that both United Airlines
and Alaska Airlines

00:02:31.770 --> 00:02:34.500
announced they'd found loose
bolts on their aircraft-

00:02:34.500 --> 00:02:37.428
- What we saw was airlines
saying, "You know what?

00:02:37.428 --> 00:02:41.520
We're gonna check our aircraft,
our Boeing MAX aircraft,

00:02:41.520 --> 00:02:43.419
even though they're not the models

00:02:43.419 --> 00:02:46.260
that the FAA has stipulated
need to be checked."

00:02:46.260 --> 00:02:48.930
- Despite having no MAX 9 aircraft,

00:02:48.930 --> 00:02:52.890
South Korea, China, and India
all ordered their airlines

00:02:52.890 --> 00:02:55.800
to examine their fleet of MAX 8 aircraft,

00:02:55.800 --> 00:02:57.180
though they found nothing.

00:02:57.180 --> 00:02:58.920
And Indonesia ordered Lion Air

00:02:58.920 --> 00:03:01.710
to inspect the doors
on its MAX 9 aircraft.

00:03:01.710 --> 00:03:05.250
- It's certainly a sign
that Boeing doesn't have

00:03:05.250 --> 00:03:08.880
full, full confidence of safety regulators

00:03:08.880 --> 00:03:10.650
or its airline customers.

00:03:10.650 --> 00:03:13.620
- That really speaks to this idea

00:03:13.620 --> 00:03:17.910
that there may be a loss of
faith, not just in Boeing,

00:03:17.910 --> 00:03:21.277
but also in Boeing's oversight by the FAA.

00:03:21.277 --> 00:03:22.385
- The FAA's administrator,
Mike Whitaker, said

00:03:22.385 --> 00:03:26.400
"The agency showed its
global leadership on safety

00:03:26.400 --> 00:03:28.500
by immediately grounding the MAX 9,"

00:03:28.500 --> 00:03:31.080
and said, "Regulators would
make sure the planes were safe

00:03:31.080 --> 00:03:32.400
before they fly again."

00:03:32.400 --> 00:03:34.320
That potential loss of faith

00:03:34.320 --> 00:03:36.120
could impact the range of planes

00:03:36.120 --> 00:03:38.550
that Boeing is able to
offer in years to come.

00:03:38.550 --> 00:03:39.660
And to understand why,

00:03:39.660 --> 00:03:43.380
you need to go back to the
MAX crashes of 2018 and 2019.

00:03:43.380 --> 00:03:46.950
- An Indonesia passenger
plane crashing into the sea

00:03:46.950 --> 00:03:48.900
minutes after takeoff.

00:03:48.900 --> 00:03:50.370
- [Reporter] Rescuers have located debris,

00:03:50.370 --> 00:03:53.790
but they do not expect
to find any survivors.

00:03:53.790 --> 00:03:56.220
- The new Boeing 737 crashing,

00:03:56.220 --> 00:03:58.500
just minutes after takeoff in Ethiopia.

00:03:58.500 --> 00:04:01.680
- 157 people from more than
30 countries were killed.

00:04:01.680 --> 00:04:04.490
- [George] The crashes of
two Boeing 737 MAX 8 aircraft

00:04:04.490 --> 00:04:06.960
led to the grounding of hundreds of jets

00:04:06.960 --> 00:04:08.640
for nearly two years.

00:04:08.640 --> 00:04:10.920
The FAA began increasing its oversight

00:04:10.920 --> 00:04:12.330
of Boeing's manufacturing

00:04:12.330 --> 00:04:14.520
and the company had to pay billions

00:04:14.520 --> 00:04:16.920
to compensate airlines
due to the MAX grounding

00:04:16.920 --> 00:04:19.170
and problems that emerged
with other aircraft.

00:04:19.170 --> 00:04:21.540
- It slowed everything down.

00:04:21.540 --> 00:04:25.140
It took up resources, it took up time.

00:04:25.140 --> 00:04:28.470
And so Boeing arguably
wasn't able to focus

00:04:28.470 --> 00:04:30.450
on developing the next new aircraft

00:04:30.450 --> 00:04:31.980
in a way they could have been.

00:04:31.980 --> 00:04:33.450
- [George] Fast forward to 2023,

00:04:33.450 --> 00:04:36.510
and Boeing now has a far
narrower selection of planes

00:04:36.510 --> 00:04:38.430
than European rival Airbus,

00:04:38.430 --> 00:04:41.490
who's offering ranges
from the 100-seat A220,

00:04:41.490 --> 00:04:45.030
all the way up to the massive A350-1000.

00:04:45.030 --> 00:04:47.220
Airbus also has the A321NEO,

00:04:47.220 --> 00:04:49.710
which can fly a greater
number of passengers farther

00:04:49.710 --> 00:04:51.930
than Boeing's 737 MAX 10,

00:04:51.930 --> 00:04:53.783
a plane which still isn't certified

00:04:53.783 --> 00:04:56.430
because of delays related
to the MAX crisis.

00:04:56.430 --> 00:04:59.220
And that's partly why
Airbus, who overtook Boeing

00:04:59.220 --> 00:05:01.380
as the world's largest
plane maker in 2019,

00:05:01.380 --> 00:05:04.860
was able to post record orders in 2023,

00:05:04.860 --> 00:05:06.840
far outstripping Boeing's orders.

00:05:06.840 --> 00:05:07.890
- And there's a question here.

00:05:07.890 --> 00:05:10.500
Does this difference between the orders

00:05:10.500 --> 00:05:11.670
speak to maybe the fact

00:05:11.670 --> 00:05:13.890
that Boeing's management are distracted?

00:05:13.890 --> 00:05:15.570
You know, they're focused on other things.

00:05:15.570 --> 00:05:17.160
Where Airbus is able to go out there

00:05:17.160 --> 00:05:18.990
and just focused on winning orders

00:05:18.990 --> 00:05:20.610
and, you know, working with customers

00:05:20.610 --> 00:05:23.980
and being able to deliver a
more nuanced product lineup

00:05:23.980 --> 00:05:25.380
that can really take away

00:05:25.380 --> 00:05:27.000
some of the market share that Boeing has.

00:05:27.000 --> 00:05:29.130
- So even though the plane landed safely,

00:05:29.130 --> 00:05:32.490
could the Alaska Airlines
incident disrupt the duopoly

00:05:32.490 --> 00:05:34.920
between Boeing and Airbus in the long run?

00:05:34.920 --> 00:05:36.630
Well, both Airbus and Boeing

00:05:36.630 --> 00:05:38.670
have huge manufacturing backlogs.

00:05:38.670 --> 00:05:41.250
So Airbus is unlikely to
gain much of an advantage

00:05:41.250 --> 00:05:42.120
in the short term.

00:05:42.120 --> 00:05:44.670
- They're pretty much
sold out for the next,

00:05:44.670 --> 00:05:46.200
you know, eight to nine years.

00:05:46.200 --> 00:05:49.440
If you wanna buy a big chunk
of new orders from Airbus,

00:05:49.440 --> 00:05:51.780
you're really looking at the early 2030s

00:05:51.780 --> 00:05:53.640
before Airbus can even
start delivering them.

00:05:53.640 --> 00:05:57.120
- And handling the MAX crisis
may have made Boeing better

00:05:57.120 --> 00:05:58.860
at dealing with its current woes.

00:05:58.860 --> 00:05:59.788
- We're seeing the company

00:05:59.788 --> 00:06:02.400
already start to do things differently.

00:06:02.400 --> 00:06:05.160
They've said that they will
open their factories up

00:06:05.160 --> 00:06:09.330
to airlines if the airlines
wanna send crews in

00:06:09.330 --> 00:06:10.770
to inspect the process.

00:06:10.770 --> 00:06:12.630
- But how Boeing goes
about restoring faith

00:06:12.630 --> 00:06:14.610
in its products and its manufacturing

00:06:14.610 --> 00:06:16.110
over the next few months

00:06:16.110 --> 00:06:19.860
could dramatically reshape the
company in the years to come.

00:06:19.860 --> 00:06:22.560
- If they're able to
come back very strongly,

00:06:22.560 --> 00:06:24.000
confident in the fixes,

00:06:24.000 --> 00:06:26.190
show that they have, you know,

00:06:26.190 --> 00:06:28.356
not only addressed the underlying issues,

00:06:28.356 --> 00:06:31.410
but manage to win back the confidence

00:06:31.410 --> 00:06:34.290
of the global industry,
maybe this is just a blip.

00:06:34.290 --> 00:06:36.660
- But if there's a lack
of faith in the company

00:06:36.660 --> 00:06:39.840
and its products, we
could see Boeing's orders

00:06:39.840 --> 00:06:41.400
and its position in the market,

00:06:41.400 --> 00:06:43.593
start to slip further in the long run.

